# hash-frx runtime dependencies.
#
# Pulled from the Fractalyze package index alongside upstream PyPI:
#   pip install -r requirements.in \
#     --extra-index-url https://fractalyze.github.io/pypi/simple/
#
# hash-frx is application-agnostic, so it deliberately does NOT depend on any
# proving-scheme, signature-scheme, or chain package. zk_dtypes (native
# finite-field dtypes) arrives via frx but is pinned here directly, and the two
# must move together: the frx extension-field lowering reads
# `efinfo.modulus_low_coeffs` (zk_dtypes >= 0.0.6), and this frx build registers
# the binary-field dtypes (tower t0..t7 + flat GHASH) in the frontend, which
# requires zk_dtypes >= 0.0.10.
#
# The pin is exact rather than that floor because a bzlmod consumer mirrors it:
# such a consumer resolves this module's pip dependencies from this lock and its
# own from its own, and only equal versions keep one zk_dtypes on a test's
# sys.path. So the pin may run ahead of the floor to satisfy a consumer, and
# lowering it back to the floor is not a cleanup.
#
# The frx pin must satisfy the `frx>=` floor in pyproject.toml (see the
# rationale there).
#
# `blake3` is the BLAKE3 team's own Rust binding, and the one runtime dependency
# here that is not frx or numpy. It backs the `HostBlake3*` rows, which need a
# native BLAKE3 the way the other host rows need `hashlib` — the standard library
# ships `blake2b`/`blake2s` and will not ship BLAKE3. Taking it rather than the
# in-tree `blake3/testing/reference.py` oracle is deliberate twice over: the
# oracle is test-only code and orders of magnitude slower than the device
# dispatch a host row exists to beat, and a differential test against this tree's
# own transcription of the spec proves far less than one against the reference
# the published vectors come from (`docs/reference/conventions.md`, byte-exactness).
absl-py
numpy
blake3
